  "slug": "segmentation-self-supervised-49da",
  "summary": "Reference-free quality metrics and cross-modality benchmarks now test whether generalist segmenters transfer to tissue and volumetric data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is direct evaluation of generalist segmentation models rather than assumption of transfer. Foundation models including Cellpose-SAM, microSAM, CellSAM, Mesmer and InstanSeg are benchmarked on live microscopy and CODEX spatial-omics data, and CRISP compares five segmentation algorithms across ten mouse tissues on Xenium. The new element is reference-free assessment: CRISP introduces a ground-truth-free purity metric for comparing segmentation quality across organs. Volumetric extension is addressed in two items: a fine-tuned Cellpose-SAM segments gold nanoparticles and cells in SBF-SEM tumor spheroid data, while a parametric physics-based framework generates 3D organoid fluorescence images with exact cell and nucleus masks for pipeline development. A semi-automated ImageJ and CellProfiler pipeline for lysosome positioning is related methodologically but addresses a distinct problem.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-27"
}
